INT. TAXIDERMY SHOP - BACK ROOM - DAWN

Glass eyes. Hundreds. Foxes, owls, one bewildered ostrich.

INSPECTOR ADAEZE OKAFOR (50s, raincoat, tired eyes) stands over a workbench. A half-stuffed heron. Fresh stitches.

ELIAS VANTERPOOL (60s, apron, ink-stained thumbs) wipes his hands. Slowly. Too slowly.

OKAFOR
Nice heron.

ELIAS
Customer's. Collects Thursday.

OKAFOR
Thursday.

She lifts the heron's wing. Peers inside. Sets it down.

OKAFOR (CONT'D)
Mr. Vanterpool, where were you Sunday night?

ELIAS
Here. Working.

OKAFOR
Alone?

ELIAS
Always alone. Animals don't gossip.

OKAFOR
Mrs. Delacroix did. Before she vanished.

ELIAS
Mrs. Delacroix gossiped about everyone.

OKAFOR
She said you kept a second ledger.

ELIAS
Everyone keeps a second ledger.

OKAFOR
Not everyone keeps it inside a badger.

Silence. Elias glances at the shelf. Okafor follows his look. Back to him.

ELIAS
You've no warrant.

OKAFOR
Door was open.

ELIAS
Door was closed.

OKAFOR
Then someone opened it. Funny, that.

ELIAS
Get out of my shop.

OKAFOR
She paid you for the badger. Didn't she? Forty years of payments. For something that isn't a badger at all.

ELIAS
Get out.

OKAFOR
Where is she, Elias?

Elias lunges for the shelf. Okafor tackles him. They crash into the workbench. The heron topples. Glass eyes rattle.

They roll. Elias grabs a scalpel. Okafor pins his wrist. They freeze, nose to nose, breath ragged.

The badger falls from the shelf. Splits open. Paper spills across the floor.

Both stare at it. Neither moves.
